 # Cell projection
 
-Also called: Projected Tetrahedra, Polyhedron Projection, Cell-based Volume Rendering
+Also called: Projected Polyhedra, Projected Tetrahedra (when restricted to tetrahedral meshes), Cell-based volume rendering
 
 ## Overview
-Cell projection is a direct volume rendering technique that decomposes volumetric data into geometric primitives (cells), projects them onto the 2D image plane, and rasterizes the resulting polygons using hardware-accelerated scan-conversion [S1][S4, book]. It solves the problem of rendering irregular and curvilinear grids with high accuracy by treating the volume as a set of opaque or semi-transparent polygons that must be composited in back-to-front order [S1][S4, book]. Unlike ray casting, which computes ray-cell intersections per pixel, cell projection computes cell-pixel intersections per cell, leveraging the efficiency of the graphics pipeline while requiring a visibility sort to handle transparency correctly [S1][S4, book].
+Cell projection is a direct volume rendering technique that treats volumetric data as a set of geometric primitives (cells), projects them onto the 2D image plane, and rasterizes the resulting polygons using hardware-accelerated or software-based scan-conversion [S1][S4, book][S2][S3, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of sequential ray casting by leveraging the efficiency of rasterization pipelines, while differing from particle-based methods (like splatting) by maintaining explicit geometric boundaries [S1][S2]. Unlike object-order ray casting, it strictly separates the projection/scan-conversion phase from the global composition phase to reduce asynchronous communication overhead in parallel implementations [S4, book].
 
