 ### CellFast
-#### definition
-A software algorithm for interactive unstructured volume rendering that moves computations for order-independent transparency and volume scan conversion from CPU to graphics hardware, eliminating the need for a software sorting pass [S7, unofficial].
-#### step
-The algorithm follows these steps: I. Preprocess dataset. For a new viewpoint: II. Visibility sort cells. For every cell in sorted back-to-front order: III. Test plane equations to determine class (1,2,3,4). IV. Project and split cell unique frontback faces. V. Compute color and opacity for thick vertex. VI. (Hardware) Scanconvert new triangles [S7, unofficial].
-#### pitfall
-In the original CellFast implementation on OpenGL accelerated desktops, Steps II-V (visibility sorting, plane equation testing, projection/splitting, and color/opacity computation) saturate the host CPU, leaving the graphics hardware running below capacity [S7, unofficial].
-#### number
-In experiments with CellFast, an average of 3.4 triangles are written per tetrahedron during the splitting process [S7, unofficial].
+A software algorithm for interactive unstructured volume rendering that moves computations for order-independent transparency and volume scan conversion from CPU to graphics hardware, eliminating the need for a software sorting pass [S7, unofficial]. The algorithm follows these steps: I. Preprocess dataset. For a new viewpoint: II. Visibility sort cells. For every cell in sorted back-to-front order: III. Test plane equations to determine class (1,2,3,4). IV. Project and split cell unique frontback faces. V. Compute color and opacity for thick vertex. VI. (Hardware) Scanconvert new triangles. In the original CellFast implementation on OpenGL accelerated desktops, Steps II-V (visibility sorting, plane equation testing, projection/splitting, and color/opacity computation) saturate the host CPU, leaving the graphics hardware running below capacity [S7, unofficial]. In experiments with CellFast, an average of 3.4 triangles are written per tetrahedron during the splitting process [S7, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Cell projection was developed at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory by Peter Williams, Claudio Silva, and Richard Max [S1, S2]. It is one of the four main paradigms of volume rendering, alongside ray casting, splatting, and shear-warp [S3, unofficial]. The method was introduced to handle curvilinear and unstructured grids where regular ray marching is difficult or inefficient [S1][S4, book]. It works directly on general grids by projecting cells onto the image plane, where the projections of the edges divide the image plane into polygons that are scan-converted and composited by standard graphics hardware [S4, book]. The technique requires a global visibility sort to composite cells in back-to-front order [S1].
+Cell projection was developed at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory by Peter Williams, Claudio Silva, Richard Cook, and Peter Max [S1, S2]. It is one of the four main paradigms of volume rendering, alongside raycasting, splatting, and shear-warp [S3, unofficial]. The method was designed to handle curvilinear and unstructured grids directly, avoiding the need for resampling [S1][S4, book]. It addresses the challenge of non-planar faces in curvilinear grids by subdividing "problem cells" into tetrahedra with planar faces [S4, book]. The technique has been parallelized for distributed-memory and shared-memory systems to handle large datasets [S1, S2][S4, book].
 
